      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "4d951230-af67-535d-bbba-7c0bfa07001b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Grigory Margulis, Leonid Levin, Russell Impagliazzo, Michael Luby\n- Concepts: Margulis lemma, leftover hash lemma, ergodic theory, diophantine approximation, lattices in Lie groups, differential geometry, cryptography\n- Topics: discrete subgroups of isometries, non-positively curved Riemannian manifolds, hyperbolic n-space\n\nFacts:\n- Grigory Margulis was born on February 24, 1946.\n- Leonid Levin was born on November 2, 1948.\n- Grigory Margulis is a Russian-American mathematician known for his work on lattices in Lie groups and for introducing methods from ergodic theory into diophantine approximation.\n- Leonid Levin is a Soviet-American computer scientist.\n- The Margulis lemma is named after Grigory Margulis.\n- The Margulis lemma is a result in differential geometry about discrete subgroups of isometries of non-positively curved Riemannian manifolds, including hyperbolic n-space.\n- The leftover hash lemma is a lemma in cryptography first stated by Russell Impagliazzo, Leonid Levin, and Michael Luby.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "8a6bee6d-a6c1-53af-8947-b37a48ba1f63",
        "text": "Page: Grigory Margulis. Sentence: Gregori Aleksandrovich Margulis (Russian: Григо́рий Алекса́ндрович Маргу́лис , first name often given as Gregory, Grigori or Grigory; born February 24, 1946) is a Russian-American mathematician known for his work on lattices in Lie groups, and the introduction of methods from ergodic theory into diophantine approximation.\nPage: Leonid Levin. Sentence: Leonid Anatolievich Levin ( ; Russian: Леони́д Анато́льевич Ле́вин ; Ukrainian: Леоні́д Анато́лійович Ле́він ; born November 2, 1948) is a Soviet-American computer scientist.\nPage: Margulis lemma. Sentence: In differential geometry, a subfield of mathematics, the Margulis lemma (named after Grigory Margulis) is a result about discrete subgroups of isometries of a non-positively curved Riemannian manifolds (e.g. the hyperbolic n-space).\nPage: Leftover hash lemma. Sentence: The leftover hash lemma is a lemma in cryptography first stated by Russell Impagliazzo, Leonid Levin, and Michael Luby.",
